00:01:58.410 --> 00:01:59.690
There's a lot of debate

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:02.270
about whether working
from home is good or bad.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:05.200
In general, most employees
prefer to work from home,

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.730
but there are downsides.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:09.630
One study found that 70%
of remote professionals

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.680
work weekends, and 45% work more

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.820
than they did before the pandemic.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.360
All the time we saved on the commute

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.940
got funneled straight back into work.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.810
This has led to an even blurrier
line between work and life,

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.670
because both now occupy the same space.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.560
A lot of workers are now
expected to be accessible 24/7.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.570
That, in addition to
missing your work friends

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:31.780
and your water cooler gossip,

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.410
has resulted in a lot of burned out,

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.290
depressed and dissociated people

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.760
trying not to lose their minds
in their living room offices.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.900
But working from home isn't all bad.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.870
For one, it saves everyone money.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.740
Employees don't have to pay for gas

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.320
or lunch from Panda Express,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.790
and employers don't have to
worry about office spaces

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:51.460
and stocking up on industrial
rolls of toilet paper.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:52.900
Although, your boss should be paying

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.070
to outfit your home office,

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.270
but that's a story for another day.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:58.060
Employees are also more productive

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.210
than they were pre-pandemic,

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.330
because they're under
less pressure to look busy

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.020
and aren't constantly being interrupted

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:06.320
by guys who won't stop
talking about body hacking.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.410
For most workers, this is reason enough

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:09.520
to keep working at home.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.800
But odds are your boss disagrees.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.530
While most employees aren't in a hurry

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.100
to rush back to a soulless
husk of an office building,

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:17.770
execs and higher level management

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.030
are pushing to get them
back in their cubicles.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:22.420
The central argument is that remote work

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:24.650
lacks a certain je ne sais qua.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.807
J.P. Morgan CEO Jamie
Dimon summed it up as such:

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:30.250
"It doesn't work for
those who want to hustle.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.970
It doesn't work for
spontaneous idea generation.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.860
It doesn't work for culture."

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.410
Which made us wonder,
is the in-person office

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.070
really all it's cracked up to be?

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:41.970
Do they actually promote
hustle, creativity, and culture?

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.720
What are employers seeing that we aren't?

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.040
Dimon's quote reflects
a broader preoccupation

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.450
among the higher-ups, namely hustle.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:50.910
This isn't anything new.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:53.210
Executives have been concerned
about their employees

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:55.380
performing with gusto since forever.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.450
To understand why, meet
Frederick Winslow Taylor,

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.660
an early 20th century mechanical engineer

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:01.750
at Bethlehem Steel.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.340
You see, season one of
the Industrial Revolution

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.540
wasn't the best as far as attendance

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:07.960
and productivity were concerned.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.380
Early factories were designed
after workhouses and prisons,

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.810
so, understandably, no
one wanted to be there.

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:16.060
Plus, workers used to the flexibilities

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:19.210
afforded by working on farms
or in small family businesses

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:22.100
had never had to clock in for
their boss at 9:00 before.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.420
Now, all of a sudden,
they had to skip Christmas

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.580
to shovel coal into a furnace all day.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:28.800
- It's Christmas Day.
You gave me the day off.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:29.980
- Would I do a thing like that?

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.510
- Taylor observed this and concluded

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:33.840
that workers were inherently lazy.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.800
His solution? Optimization.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:37.820
So, like a little industrial gremlin,

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.220
he crawled around the factory

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.290
and timed workers with a stopwatch,

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:42.870
looking for any wasted movements

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.590
to shave off their routines.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:46.290
Taylor's goal was to make workers

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:47.950
as machine-like as possible.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:51.100
They went from performing
complex processes on their own

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.960
to only being responsible
for a single step.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:56.640
It was more soul-sucking than
making out with a Dementor.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.610
Industrialist Henry Ford
eventually used Taylor's ideas

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:00.840
to maximize productivity

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.280
via the infamous Ford assembly line.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.140
Taylor found that the
more data he collected

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.060
and the more control he
had over the process,

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.800
the better workers performed.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.410
But to make this function smoothly,

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.190
he needed a steady supply of loyal workers

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:15.090
committed to the business hierarchy,

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:17.610
so he created the middle manager.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.100
Originally, they took the
form of disciplinarians

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.300
and/or gang bosses.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.150
As middle managers spread
across the workplace,

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:25.370
they kept watch, fined or expelled workers

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:28.660
for nefarious offenses,
such as talking and eating,

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.760
and made sure all the
mind-numbing activities

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.790
were done on time, or else.

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:35.060
Around the 1930s, people
started coming around

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:38.030
to the then novel idea that
workers performed better

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:40.280
when they weren't afraid of
being punched in the head.

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.660
Middle managers' job descriptions expanded

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:44.800
from maintaining productivity at all costs

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.017
to also asking concerned questions like,

00:05:47.017 --> 00:05:48.070
"You hangin' in there?"

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:49.820
This shift created the modern manager.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:52.550
But despite their growing
concern for employee welfare,

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:55.550
they're not all warm handshakes
and pats on the back.

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:56.460
And thanks to Taylor,

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.550
there's still a narrative
that workers are innately lazy

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.970
and managers continue to
fixate on surveillance

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:03.880
as a way of controlling productivity.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.140
At the start of the pandemic,

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:08.800
use of surveillance
software increased by 50%

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.110
and continues to rise
in spite of productivity

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.120
being at an all-time high.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.090
The software doesn't
just give your employer

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:16.990
the ability to look through your email

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:18.600
and the memes you post on Slack.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:20.680
- They can control your webcam and mic,

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.230
take screenshots of your desktop,

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:25.100
and monitor your activity
down to the keystroke.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:28.370
So, you know, like Big Brother
(beep) but absolutely real.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.220
And this is a natural evolution

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.770
for an American management system

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:34.610
built on not trusting
people to do their jobs.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:36.610
And how do you monitor
a big group of people

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:38.250
who you think are lazy babies?

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:41.270
You schedule a whole lot of meetings.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:43.570
Indeed, meetings have increased
in length and frequency

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:44.940
over the past 50 years,

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.770
to the extent that executives on average

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:49.020
spend over half their work week in them.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.950
This wouldn't be an issue if
meetings were actually helpful,

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:54.010
but more often than not employees report

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.800
they're unproductive,
inefficient and disruptive.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:00.090
During the pandemic, managers
scheduled even more meetings.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:02.630
It sure seems like what the
anti-work-from-home crew

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:06.230
is missing isn't work but
the optics of doing work.

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:09.470
Okay, so maybe offices and
the people who run them

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:11.770
are built on some serious trust issues.

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:14.480
But what about the
spontaneous idea generation

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:16.040
that's rumored to happen at the office?

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:17.570
Do we need to be in person

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:19.630
to harness that sweet, sweet synergy?

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:22.310
Not if you ask the people
designing our offices.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:25.970
Currently 70-80% of American
offices are open plan.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:28.220
While many attribute
this innovative design

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:29.920
to Silicon Valley tech companies,

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:32.020
they're the dirty work of, you guessed it,

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.920
our good friend Frederick Taylor.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:36.220
He arranged workers in
endless rows of desks

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:39.300
with offices lining the outside
for managers to keep watch.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:41.730
The open office plan was
hailed as a huge success,

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:44.220
because it improved
productivity, efficiency,

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:46.870
and made surveilling your
workforce even easier.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.780
On the downside, it
encouraged noise, distractions

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.750
and tons of animosity towards the guy

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.210
chewing a tuna fish sandwich
with his mouth open.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.790
Believe it or not, cubicles
were originally designed

00:07:56.790 --> 00:07:59.600
as a solution to the chaotic
grind of the open office.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.350
But through a series of cost cuts,

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:04.100
offices quickly devolved
into cubicle farms.

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:06.620
So companies swerved back
to the open office plan,

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:08.100
which is conveniently cheaper,

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:09.710
because they don't have to buy any walls,

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:13.000
and supposedly more conducive
to happy collaboration.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.290
But it turns out that open
floor plans have been found

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:17.910
to almost universally
increase stress levels,

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.630
heighten blood pressure,
as well as cause burnout.

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:23.200
Also, open offices are downright terrible

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:25.610
for spontaneous
communication and creativity.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:27.150
Surveys find that open offices

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:30.850
actually resulted in 70% less
face-to-face communication,

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:34.590
and use of electronic
messaging increased by 22-50%.

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:37.620
Most people just plug in their
headphones, blast some BTS,

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:39.910
and do their best to
tune everyone else out.

00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:42.790
And tuning people out is hard
when nobody has any privacy

00:08:42.790 --> 00:08:44.640
and where Steve in the sales department

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:46.640
has a super loud phone voice.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.700
All these interruptions mean less time

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:50.500
for the distraction-free concentration

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:52.360
that actually leads to good ideas.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:54.430
The more companies try
to control creativity,

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:56.220
the more they actually snuff it out.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.500
So why are executives pushing for this?

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:01.430
Because they generally don't
work in an open office.

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:03.240
They have their own private offices

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.880
with walls and doors
and space and privacy.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:07.430
The office that they want to return to

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:09.190
is significantly less (beep)

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:11.290
than the office their
employees will return to.

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:12.890
You'd want to go back to the office, too,

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:14.560
if you had a private beverage fridge

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:16.140
and assistants to grab your lunch.

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:19.110
But we also have to admit
that, at least for some of us,

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:21.990
working from home can
be incredibly isolating.

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:23.370
It's easy to feel disconnected

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:25.010
from your coworkers and your company

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:27.290
when you only interact
with them through a screen,

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:28.530
especially when you find out

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:30.600
they were much taller than you thought.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.310
Maybe going into work is worth it

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:33.620
to preserve company culture,

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:36.260
but what even is company culture?

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:38.820
The idea of corporate culture was predated

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.960
by a period of economic
stagnation in the '70s

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:44.570
that gave rise to a historic
amount of downsizing

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:45.680
in the '80s and '90s.

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:48.550
This resulted in a huge
wave of corporate cynicism,

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:50.250
as employees realized that companies

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:52.670
no longer had their
best interests at heart.

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:55.490
Organization consultants
Terence Deal and Allan Kennedy

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.470
tried to repair this rift between
corporations and employees

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:00.940
with their book "The
New Corporate Cultures".

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:03.830
They promised that a business
could cut costs, stay lean,

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:05.950
and make their employees less miserable

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:07.540
with company culture.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:09.010
Business schools ate this up

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:10.980
like it was thousand-dollar caviar.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.530
More democratic practices were introduced,

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:16.090
like treating employees
like responsible adults,

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:19.330
in addition to team building
events, more cake and parties.

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:21.340
The company was a family again;

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:23.230
that is, if your family is ruthless enough

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:25.900
to (beep) can you while everyone
else is doing goat yoga.

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:27.020
And as much as they claim

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:29.320
they aren't like other mega-corporations,

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:31.770
venture capitalists
promoted the exact same

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:34.790
toxic company culture, just
with different frosting.

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:37.620
Startups used the idea that
an app could change the world

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:39.980
to encourage obsessive
devotion to the company

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:41.400
and to justify the destruction

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.720
of any semblance of work-life balance.

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:45.803
- This isn't just my job.

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:47.960
This is my religion.

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:49.630
- Even if you don't work at one of these

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:53.850
IPA-spewing mega corporations,
company culture still sucks.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:54.930
An organization's goal

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:57.350
will always be profit
and optimization first,

00:10:57.350 --> 00:11:01.210
employee health and stability
second, or third, or fourth.

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:03.050
What's more, the company culture movement

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:05.320
hasn't exactly made
traditional office spaces

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:06.720
better at being inclusive.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.610
After all, traditional offices
were specifically designed

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:12.530
with the comfort of usually
white male executives in mind,

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:16.060
right down to the standardized
68 to 76 degree temperatures,

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:17.760
because sweaty suits.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:18.910
At the end of the workday,

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:21.770
company culture is just an
enticing emotional narrative

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:23.440
that makes workers invest more time

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:24.790
and energy into the company.

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:27.570
It's no surprise that the main
defendants of company culture

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.140
are largely the same executives

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:30.850
pushing to go back to the office,

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:33.440
because the office has
always worked best for them.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:35.260
But much to your boss's dismay,

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:36.610
offices probably aren't going back

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:37.820
to the way they used to be,

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:39.660
or at least not across the board.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:42.530
We might just be on the brink
of an exciting turning point.

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:44.830
Ongoing remote work is
changing everything,

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:46.910
even the very shape of our cities.

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:49.530
Mark Muro, a Senior
Fellow and Policy Director

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:52.170
at the Brookings institution,
noted that remote workers

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:54.420
are moving out of major
metropolitan areas,

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:57.030
like New York or the Bay
Area, to the suburbs,

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:59.640
creating a donut-shaped population density

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:00.670
around major cities.

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:02.920
Workers wanna be close enough
to commute to the office

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:03.753
a couple days a week

00:12:03.753 --> 00:12:07.070
but still gain the financial
benefits of suburban life.

00:12:07.070 --> 00:12:10.020
The influx of remote workers
to suburbs and small cities

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:11.980
is both good and bad.

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:14.430
On the one hand, remote workers
support the local economy

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:16.570
by working out of coffee shops, cafes,

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:18.040
and other mixed-use spaces.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:20.830
On the other hand, the influx
of people making city money

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:22.770
snapping up houses in smaller markets

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:26.220
are causing housing prices to
rise at unprecedented rates.

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:28.110
Workers supported by the local economy

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:29.610
are being priced outta town.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:31.560
Plus, many small cities and suburbs

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:34.080
simply were not built to
hold this many people.

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:35.380
They don't have the infrastructure,

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:37.480
like transportation and available housing,

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:39.630
to support the sudden
increase in population.

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:42.130
This is contributing to
a growing class divide,

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:45.430
which remote urban workers were
originally trying to escape.

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:47.450
Speaking of big metropolitan areas,

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:48.940
are they just a lost cause?

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:50.700
Culture journalists Charlie Warzel

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:52.430
and Anne Helen Petersen don't think so.

00:12:52.430 --> 00:12:54.540
They argue that, because
work in the office

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.570
is no longer an incentive to live in town,

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:58.680
city officials should
work with urban planners

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:00.040
to step up their game.

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:02.180
Large office buildings can be transformed

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:03.650
into multi-use spaces.

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:05.630
Cities may shift from giving tax subsidies

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:07.190
for new factories and warehouses

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:09.630
to investing in schools,
parks, and the arts

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:11.600
to encourage residents to stick around.

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:13.960
It could also mean
investing in street dining,

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:15.840
better transit, improved walkability,

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:17.320
and more flexible workspaces

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:20.100
for people who don't want to
work at home or the office.

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:22.940
In essence, cities may move
from appeasing job makers

00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:26.340
to greasing the palms of the
people actually doing the work.

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:30.240
But no matter where we are,
we still have a long way to go

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:32.390
in negotiating our boundaries with work.

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:35.290
As we become more spatially
decentralized from the office,

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:36.680
maybe it's time to make work

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:39.060
more decentralized from our lives.

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:40.020
But what do you guys think?

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:42.600
What if work wasn't the
center of our lives and homes?

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:43.970
Could working remotely be the key

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:46.000
to escaping our corporate overlords

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:47.960
and real pants once and for all?

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:49.540
Let us know what you
think in the comments.

